### 3\. The actual reconstruction problems you need to solve

With that architecture in place, there are several distinct problems to tackle.

### A. Recovering heading hierarchy

Very high priority

This is one of the clearest research findings.

Recognising that something is a heading is not the same as knowing what level of heading it is.

For example:

```text
PART I
    1. Definitions
        1.1 Interpretation
            (a) General
                (i) Application
```

The output should be:

```markdown
# PART I

## 1. Definitions

### 1.1 Interpretation

#### (a) General

##### (i) Application
```

A document parser may correctly recognise every one of these headings but put them all at level one.

MPDocBench-Parse found that heading hierarchy recovery remains difficult, particularly in magazines, newspapers, books and reports where the structure is less explicit. arxiv.org +1

There is, however, a promising practical approach.

Docling has introduced a heading-hierarchy stage that uses three signals, in order:

* PDF bookmarks and embedded table of contents.

* Numbering patterns in headings.

* Visual formatting, including font size, weight, italics and capitalisation.

Interestingly, this stage is disabled by default in the documented pipeline. Docling +1

I'd investigate enabling it, subject to your pinned Docling version supporting it.

I'd also extend your existing chapter extraction methodology.

You already have a useful deterministic process in `chapters.py`: it looks for a table of contents, matches entries against the document body, then falls back to heading structures and chapter markers. GitHub +1

At present, that's principally being used to split the document into chapters.

I'd elevate it into a document-structure discovery process.

In particular, don't just recover the top-level chapters. Recover nested TOC entries and their hierarchy, then match them to the actual headings throughout the document.

This would give you an independent source of evidence for the heading tree.

### B. Reconstructing paragraphs across pages

Very high priority

This problem is deceptively difficult.

Imagine the original document:

Page 12

> The Australian economy experienced significant structural changes during the reporting period, particularly in relation to international trade and investment.

Page 13

> These developments resulted in a corresponding change in the distribution of economic activity.

A naive page-by-page converter might produce two paragraphs.

But the correct representation might be one continuous paragraph.

There are actually several distinct cases:

* A paragraph continues onto the next page.

* A paragraph continues in another column.

* A sentence has been interrupted by a page break.

* A word has been hyphenated at the end of a line.

* A completely new paragraph begins at the top of the following page.

These need to be distinguished.

MPDocBench-Parse explicitly evaluates truncated-text merging and finds that semantic continuity is still a substantial weakness, even for advanced document parsers. arxiv.org +2

I'd introduce a continuation-detection stage.

It could use:

* Whether the preceding block ends in sentence punctuation.

* Whether the following block begins with a continuation-like phrase.

* Indentation and paragraph formatting.

* Font and text-style consistency.

* Page and column position.

* Whether the two blocks belong to the same section.

* Whether the previous line ends with a hyphen.

One particularly important detail: don't automatically remove hyphens at line endings.

For example:

`inter-` followed by `national`

could represent a line-break hyphenation of international, but it could also be an actual printed hyphen in a word.

Retain the original transcription and make any reconstruction a separately recorded operation.

### C. Reconstructing tables across pages

Very high priority

You already have table-row reconstruction in Wiseau, but I would regard that as the first stage of a much larger table problem.

You need to understand:

* Which text belongs to which cell.

* Whether a cell spans multiple columns or rows.

* Whether multiple visually separate tables are actually one table.

* Whether a table continues across pages.

* Whether repeated column headings should be removed when joining pages.

* Whether notes and captions belong to the table.

* Whether the table contains nested headings or grouped data.

The MPDocBench researchers found that merging tables across pages remains particularly difficult. Even when systems identify the correct table fragments, they can still reconstruct the resulting cell structure incorrectly. arxiv.org +1

This brings me to another important point about Markdown.

Standard Markdown tables cannot faithfully represent every table.

Docling's own documentation explicitly confirms that its Markdown serializer flattens merged cells, whereas its JSON and HTML serializers preserve their row and column spans. Docling +1

GitHub Flavoured Markdown also doesn't support `rowspan` or `colspan` in its table syntax. MDN

I would adopt a simple rule:

| Source table                                                          | Markdown representation                                                                  |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Simple rectangular table                                              | Standard Markdown table                                                                  |
| Merged heading cells                                                  | HTML table embedded in Markdown                                                          |
| Complex nested cells                                                  | HTML table                                                                               |
| Table continuing across pages                                         | Reconstructed as one table                                                               |
| Table with complicated layout that cannot be faithfully reconstructed | Preserve the original table image and provide a structured representation where possible |

Don't sacrifice the source's structure simply to make everything fit into pipe-delimited Markdown.

### D. Figures, photographs, diagrams and charts

High priority

This is an area that I think many PDF-to-Markdown tools underestimate.

A photograph isn't just an image sitting between two paragraphs.

It might have:

* A caption.

* A figure number.

* A source attribution.

* A note.

* A reference in the body text.

* Several subfigures, labelled A, B and C.

The output should preserve these relationships.

LongDocBench specifically evaluates relationships between figures and tables and their captions, notes and sources. Its dataset contains 3,258 such relationships, including many one-to-many associations. arxiv.org

My recommendation is to extract images as separate assets and reference them from Markdown.

For example:

```markdown
![Figure 4](assets/figure-004.png)

**Figure 4.** Distribution of employment by industry.

_Source: Australian Bureau of Statistics._
```

I would also preserve the figure's page number and bounding box in the intermediate representation.

One specific observation about Wiseau: your current Docling client requests `image_export_mode=placeholder`, which deliberately prevents huge base64 images being embedded in Markdown. That's sensible, but it means the result principally records that an image was present, rather than delivering the actual image as an asset. GitHub

I'd change that to an asset-export mechanism rather than embedding base64 or simply dropping the image.

A generated description of a chart or photograph can be useful for LLM consumption, but it must be labelled as generated metadata, not presented as text that appeared in the original document.

### E. Footnotes, endnotes and references

High priority

This is easy to overlook.

Consider:

> The policy commenced in 1995.¹

A footnote at the bottom of the page might explain an important qualification.

If the conversion puts the note in the middle of the paragraph, or discards it as a page footer, the resulting Markdown is incomplete.

You need to identify:

* Footnote markers within paragraphs.

* Their corresponding footnote text.

* Endnotes and their references.

* Superscript and subscript formatting.

* Bibliographic references.

* Cross-references between figures, tables and sections.

For a compatible Markdown renderer, footnotes can be represented using an extension such as:

```markdown
The policy commenced in 1995.[^1]

[^1]: The policy was initially introduced on a trial basis.
```

For a more faithful reconstruction, maintain a reference between the marker and its note in the intermediate representation rather than relying solely on the textual numbering.

### F. Headers, footers and page numbering

I would treat these as a separate class of content called document furniture.

Repeated running headers, page numbers and footers often aren't part of the document's main narrative. But they can be useful.

For example, a report might contain:

* Document title in the header.

* Current chapter in the running header.

* Confidentiality status in the footer.

* Printed page number.

I wouldn't simply delete all of these.

I'd detect repeated elements across pages and classify them as headers or footers. Then preserve the information in the document metadata or page-level representation.

This is consistent with the way Docling separates `body` from `furniture`.

The W3C's guidance on tagged PDFs also highlights the importance of logical reading order, including the way complex page elements are sequenced for accessibility. It warns that even tagged PDFs can have reading-order problems. W3C +1

For Wiseau, I'd make the decision configurable:

* Reader-friendly output: suppress repetitive running headers and footers.

* Faithful output: preserve them as page-level annotations or explicit markers.

### 4\. Something I'd change immediately: your Markdown normalisation

This is a code-level observation rather than an academic recommendation.

I looked at `backend/parsers/cleaner.py`.

It currently performs several character replacements:

| Original | Wiseau currently converts to |
| -------- | ---------------------------- |
| ‘ and ’  | '                            |
| “ and ”  | "                            |
| –        | -                            |
| —        | --                           |
| …        | ...                          |

It also strips trailing whitespace and collapses multiple blank lines. GitHub +1

I understand why this was introduced: consistent output, predictable tokenisation and deterministic Markdown.

But I would reconsider it in light of your actual objective.

An accurate transcription should preserve the punctuation that was printed.

For example:

* `1980–1986` is not typographically identical to `1980-1986`.

* An em dash is not the same character as two hyphens.

* An ellipsis glyph is not necessarily equivalent to three independently typed periods.

* Historical quotations may use punctuation conventions that are themselves useful evidence.

There is also a small technical issue.

CommonMark recognises two trailing spaces before a newline as a hard line break. Your cleaner strips all trailing spaces. Therefore, an extractor that uses this Markdown convention to preserve line breaks could lose that formatting during normalisation. spec.commonmark.org +1

I'd separate three operations:

| Operation                 | Recommended behaviour                                              |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Text preservation         | Retain the original recognised characters                          |
| Structural reconstruction | Interpret line wrapping, paragraphs, headings and formatting       |
| Output normalisation      | Produce valid, consistent Markdown without changing source content |

Unicode NFC normalisation and line-ending standardisation are reasonable technical operations in many contexts. Rewriting punctuation is a different matter.

You could still offer an optional simplified-text output for downstream systems, but the faithful representation should be the default.

