### 6\. How I'd evaluate whether the resulting Markdown is accurate

This is another area where I would move beyond your current OCR benchmark methodology.

You currently have a fairly good framework for measuring text recognition. But you'd need another set of measurements for structural fidelity.

The research provides some useful precedents.

OmniDocBench evaluates text, layout, reading order, tables and formulas using different metrics. MPDocBench-Parse extends this into multi-page continuity and heading hierarchy. LongDocBench adds document-level relationships.

I'd adopt a similar approach.

| Dimension | Suggested measure | What you're testing |
|--------|--------|--------|
| Text fidelity | WER / CER | Is all text retained accurately? |
| Block detection | Precision / recall / F1 | Have all paragraphs, tables, headings and figures been found? |
| Reading order | Normalised edit distance | Is the sequence correct? |
| Heading hierarchy | Tree edit distance | Are sections and subsections nested correctly? |
| Table fidelity | TEDS | Are rows, columns and cells correctly reconstructed? |
| Figure relationships | Precision / recall / F1 | Are captions, sources and notes attached to the correct figures? |
| Footnote relationships | Precision / recall / F1 | Are the references correctly associated? |
| Cross-page continuity | Merge/split accuracy | Are continuing paragraphs and tables reconstructed correctly? |
| Asset preservation | Asset recall | Have all meaningful figures been retained? |

TEDS, for example, is a tree-edit-distance metric originally developed for table structure recognition. It evaluates the reconstructed table structure rather than just comparing a flattened string of words.

For heading hierarchy, MPDocBench-Parse uses tree edit distance to compare predicted heading structures with their ground-truth hierarchies.

### One particularly useful testing idea

I'd create a small collection of manually annotated *gold-standard documents*\.

But instead of manually transcribing only their words, annotate their actual structure.

For every element, record:

- Its type.
- Its text.
- Its parent.
- Its reading order.
- Its source page.
- Its relationships with other elements.

Then compare Wiseau's reconstructed document tree against that reference.

This would let you identify cases where the text is perfect but the document is structurally wrong.

I'd include a few difficult examples, especially:

- A multi-column publication.
- A report with a deeply nested section hierarchy.
- A document with several tables continuing across pages.
- A document with lots of footnotes.
- A magazine with photographs, captions and sidebars.
- A document with an unusually complicated table of contents.

You don't need hundreds of documents to begin. A carefully selected sample of perhaps 10–15 documents, with a number of manually annotated pages each, would be a useful starting point.

One other thing: retain the original page images and build a visual inspection facility. Ideally, selecting a reconstructed Markdown block should show the corresponding source region. That gives you a way to investigate errors rather than merely seeing that the final output looks wrong.

